{"metadata":{"citation_count":166,"publication_info":[{"year":2011,"artid":"030","page_start":"030","journal_title":"JCAP","journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1212538"},"journal_volume":"07"}],"citation_count_without_self_citations":127,"core":true,"dois":[{"value":"10.1088/1475-7516/2011/07/030"}],"titles":[{"title":"Shear viscous effects on the primordial power spectrum from warm inflation"},{"title":"Shear viscous effects on the primordial power spectrum from warm inflation","source":"arXiv"}],"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","authors":[{"uuid":"95b00202-cf23-49bd-b3a2-9c457350ffb9","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1017167"},"full_name":"Bastero-Gil, Mar","affiliations":[{"value":"Granada U., Theor. Phys. Astrophys.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903836"}}],"signature_block":"GALm","raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Departamento de Física Teórica y del Cosmos, Universidad de Granada, Granada-18071, Spain"}]},{"uuid":"860bb508-a5ef-40b1-b22c-e4da0a1b6f26","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1016688"},"full_name":"Berera, Arjun","affiliations":[{"value":"Edinburgh U.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/902787"}}],"signature_block":"BARARa","raw_affiliations":[{"value":"SUPA, School of Physics and Astronomy, University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh, EH9 3JZ, U.K."}]},{"uuid":"c87ce2b9-233b-4e9e-aac3-2b1d5f017d07","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/992329"},"full_name":"Ramos, Rudnei O.","affiliations":[{"value":"Rio de Janeiro State U.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/904929"}}],"signature_block":"RANr","curated_relation":true,"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Departamento de Física Teórica, Universidade do Estado do Rio de Janeiro, 20550-013 Rio de Janeiro, RJ, Brazil"}]}],"curated":true,"figures":[{"key":"2d6b4a62811a7d70b0e037d24351a12e","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/2d6b4a62811a7d70b0e037d24351a12e","source":"arxiv","caption":"Evolution of the power spectrum of the field $\\langle y_k^2\\rangle$, radiation energy density $\\langle w_k^2\\rangle$, and radiation momentum $\\langle u_k^2\\rangle/Q^2$, for $Q=100$, and wavenumber $k=10^4 H$. The vertical thin dotted line sets the value of the freeze out scale $k_F$ in warm inflation. The results are shown for different power dependence on $T$ of the dissipative coefficient: $c=3$ (solid lines), $c=1$ (dashed lines), $c=-1$ (dash-dotted lines), and $c=0$ (dotted lines).","filename":"spectra_chaot_kk10000_Q100_yy_p.png"},{"key":"eca325ca7d4d866296f0b63257113a65","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/eca325ca7d4d866296f0b63257113a65","source":"arxiv","caption":"Evolution of the total curvature perturbation spectrum $P_{\\cal R}^{1/2}$ (black lines), the radiation $P_{{\\cal R}_r}^{1/2}$ (red lines), and the field $P_{{\\cal R}_\\phi}^{1/2}$ (green lines) curvature perturbation spectrum. The results are shown for different power dependence on $T$ of the dissipative coefficient: $c=3$ (solid lines), $c=1$ (dashed lines), $c=-1$ (dash-dotted lines), and $c=0$ (dotted lines).","filename":"spectra_chaot_kk10000_Q100_Pr_p.png"},{"key":"c33a5d15d498e97a8215ed2d7eff391f","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/c33a5d15d498e97a8215ed2d7eff391f","source":"arxiv","caption":"The spectrum of the field at horizon crossing $\\langle y_k^2\\rangle_*$ as a function of the dissipative parameter $Q$, at zero order in the slow-roll parameters, for different values of $c$, and no shear $\\bar \\zeta_s=0$. Solid lines are obtained integrating Eqs. (\\ref{EOMyk})-(\\ref{EOMuk}), while dashed lines were obtained with the approximation used in \\cite{mossgraham}.","filename":"spectra_chaot_Qiter_kk100_gralT_p.png"},{"key":"71c026e17d9c48fdd3072eed1f6853bd","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/71c026e17d9c48fdd3072eed1f6853bd","source":"arxiv","caption":"noimgCoefficients for the numerical fit of the spectrum, Eq. (\\ref{yyapprox}) and Eq. (\\ref{yyapproxcn1}).","filename":"fig00003.png"},{"key":"5e40a42039f2d5dd26ac615f4a6a7f62","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5e40a42039f2d5dd26ac615f4a6a7f62","source":"arxiv","caption":"The spectrum of the field $\\langle y_k^2\\rangle$ at horizon crossing as a function of the dissipative parameter $Q_*$, for different inflationary models, $c=3$ and no shear $\\bar \\zeta_s=0$. The solid line is the result at zero order in slow-roll; dashed lines are for a chaotic model with $p=$6,4,2, from top to bottom; the dash-dotted line is a quadratic hybrid model.","filename":"ianeq_c3_k100_z1_all_p.png"},{"key":"1cfaab8cfbc4c283577f08a7362b66f7","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/1cfaab8cfbc4c283577f08a7362b66f7","source":"arxiv","caption":"Field spectrum normalized by the value with $c=0$, as a function of the shear parameter $\\bar \\zeta_s$, for different values of $c$, and $Q=40$. From top to bottom, $c=3,\\,1,\\,-1$. For each curve, we have also considered two different $T$ dependence on the shear viscosity, as indicated in the legend, but both gives the same field spectrum.","filename":"spectra_chaot_Qiter_kk100_cshear_T_p.png"},{"key":"f38b70013b144ab5ef06c7384baa459e","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/f38b70013b144ab5ef06c7384baa459e","source":"arxiv","caption":"Field spectrum as a function of $Q_*$, for a quartic chaotic model, with a cubic dissipative coefficient (Eq. \\ref{upslowT}) and $\\zeta_s$ given by Eq. (\\ref{zetaslf}). We have taken ${\\cal N}_\\sigma=10^3$ and $h=\\sqrt{4\\pi}$ (solid line), $h=1.67$ (dashed line) and $h=1.12$ (dot-dashed line). We include for comparison the result with $c=0$.","filename":"spectra_chaot_Qiter_kk100_pp4_shear_p.png"}],"license":[{"url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","imposing":"arXiv"}],"texkeys":["Bastero-Gil:2011rva","BasteroGil:2011xd"],"citeable":true,"keywords":[{"value":"05.70.","schema":"PACS"},{"value":"98.80.Cq","schema":"PACS"},{"value":"98.80.Es","schema":"PACS"}],"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"We compute the primordial curvature spectrum generated during warm inflation, including shear viscous effects. The primordial spectrum is dominated by the thermal fluctuations of the radiation bath, sourced by the dissipative term of the inflaton field. The dissipative coefficient \\Upsilon, computed from first principles in the close-to-equilibrium approximation, depends in general on the temperature T, and this dependence renders the system of the linear fluctuations coupled. Whenever the dissipative coefficient is larger than the Hubble expansion rate H, there is a growing mode in the fluctuations before horizon crossing. However, dissipation intrinsically means departures from equilibrium, and therefore the presence of a shear viscous pressure in the radiation fluid. This in turn acts as an extra friction term for the radiation fluctuations that tends to damp the growth of the perturbations. Independently of the T functional dependence of the dissipation and the shear viscosity, we find that when the shear viscous coefficient \\zeta_s is larger than 3 \\rho_r/H at horizon crossing, \\rho_r being the radiation energy density, the shear damping effect wins and there is no growing mode in the spectrum.","source":"arXiv"}],"references":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/415293"},"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"astro-ph/9601067","publication_info":{"artid":"L1","page_start":"L1","journal_title":"Astrophys.J.","journal_volume":"464"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/415294"},"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"astro-ph/9601063","publication_info":{"artid":"L11","page_start":"L11","journal_title":"Astrophys.J.","journal_volume":"464"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/526767"},"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"astro-ph/0004404","publication_info":{"artid":"955","page_start":"955","journal_title":"Nature","journal_volume":"404"}},"curated_r